2. No, this isn't feasible. The fundamental issue is that when a trap occurs, the user's page table satp is still active. The kernel stack and proc structure addresses are kernel virtual addresses, which are invalid in the user's current memory mapping. Attempting to write registers there would immediately cause a page fault.
The TRAPFRAME works because it is a page mapped into the user's address space at a known location, providing a safe, accessible landing pad to save the registers before switching to the kernel page table.

3. This is also not practical. The RISC-V stvec register must be set to the virtual address of the trap handler code. When a trap occurs, while in user mode, the CPU must be able to find this address using the user's page table. The TRAMPOLINE page is simply the mechanism xv6 uses to map this essential jump code into every user's address space. If its eliminated, you would still have to map the trap entry code into the user address space somehow, which is exactly what the TRAMPOLINE page already accomplishes.
